course: dictionary
lesson: 14895
title: Reconstruction: building again, copying the past, or working out what happened
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Reconstruction is a noun, and the start of the word is your first clue. Re- means again, and construction means building. So a reconstruction is often something built again after it was damaged.
explain: «After the flood, the town needed years of reconstruction.» The clues are flood and years: the town was damaged, and people had to rebuild it. Swap in the meaning, building it again after damage, and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: «The museum has a reconstruction of a Roman house.» Here the clues are museum and Roman. A reconstruction is a copy made to show how something looked long ago, not a real old house.
explain: «The police did a reconstruction of the crash to find out what happened.» This is a third meaning: the events are acted out step by step to work out what happened. When you meet a word with more than one meaning, pick the one that fits the sentence best.
fact: Reconstruction means building something again after it was damaged or destroyed.
fact: A reconstruction can also be a copy that shows how something looked long ago, like a model of an old village.
fact: A reconstruction can be an attempt to work out what happened, for example by acting out a crash or a crime step by step.
fact: «Under reconstruction» means being rebuilt or repaired.
fact: The prefix re- often means again, as in rebuild, rewrite and reheat.
exercise: «After the flood, the bridge needed reconstruction.» What does reconstruction mean here? | Painting it a new colour | Measuring how long it is | Swimming across it | *Building it again after damage
exercise: «The museum had a reconstruction of an old village.» What is the reconstruction in this sentence? | *A copy showing how the village looked long ago | A shop that sells old things | A real village that people still live in today | A map of the sea
exercise: What does the re- at the start of reconstruction mean? | Before | Not | Under | *Again
test: «The police held a reconstruction of the accident.» What does this suggest? | A party to celebrate the end of the accident | A picture of a bus on a wall | A new car for the police | *Acting out the events step by step to find out what happened
test: «The old school is under reconstruction.» What does under reconstruction mean? | *It is being rebuilt or repaired | It is very old and lovely | It has been closed for ever | It has been painted green
exam: «After the earthquake, the village needed a long reconstruction.» What does this mean? | *The village was being rebuilt after the damage | The village was moved to the coast | The village was being painted for a summer festival | The village was closed to visitors for ever
